\begin{proof}
Let R=\mathbb{Z}[\sqrt{2}]. Suppose, for contradiction, that x^2-\sqrt{2} is reducible over R. Since it is a monic polynomial of degree 2 over the integral domain R, it must then have a root in R. Write such a root as a+b\sqrt{2}, where a,b\in\mathbb{Z}. Then
\[
(a+b\sqrt{2})^2-\sqrt{2}=a^2+2b^2+(2ab-1)\sqrt{2}=0.
\]
Because 1 and \sqrt{2} are linearly independent over \mathbb{Z}, we obtain 2ab-1=0, so 2ab=1, which is impossible for integers a and b. Thus x^2-\sqrt{2} has no root in R and is irreducible over R.\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Set R=\mathbb{Z}[\sqrt{2}]. The ring R is an integral domain. Assume that
\[
f(x)=x^2-\sqrt{2}
\]
is reducible in R[x]. Since f has degree 2, one of its factors has degree 1, and hence f has a root r\in R. Write r=a+b\sqrt{2} with a,b\in\mathbb{Z}. The equation f(r)=0 gives
\[
a^2+2b^2+2ab\sqrt{2}=\sqrt{2}.
\]
Comparing the coefficients of 1 and \sqrt{2} yields 2ab=1, impossible because 2ab is even. Therefore f has no root in R, so it is irreducible over \mathbb{Z}[\sqrt{2}].\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let R=\mathbb{Z}[\sqrt{2}] and f(x)=x^2-\sqrt{2}. The given assumption that R is a U.F.D. implies in particular that R is an integral domain. If f were reducible in R[x], then, because \deg f=2, it would factor as a product of two degree-one polynomials and consequently have a root in R. Let r=a+b\sqrt{2}\in R be such a root, with a,b\in\mathbb{Z}. Then
\[
0=f(r)=(a+b\sqrt{2})^2-\sqrt{2}=a^2+2b^2+(2ab-1)\sqrt{2}.
\]
The elements 1 and \sqrt{2} are linearly independent over \mathbb{Z}; hence 2ab-1=0. This would imply 2ab=1, impossible for integers a,b. Thus f has no root in R, so it is irreducible in R[x]. Therefore x^2-\sqrt{2} is irreducible over \mathbb{Z}[\sqrt{2}].\end{proof}
